    <section class="guide-section" id="common-mistakes">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">06 &mdash; Common Mistakes</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">Common mistakes that cost creators brand deals</h2>
        <div class="tldr">
          <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 space-y-1">
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Biggest mistakes are strategic, not design-related</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Wrong content selection and missing information kill deals</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Treating the portfolio as a one-time project instead of a living document</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <ul class="pl-5 mb-4 space-y-2 text-ink-2">
          <li><strong>Including everything you have ever made</strong> &mdash; More is not better. A portfolio with thirty videos overwhelms the viewer and dilutes your strongest work. We consistently see that creators with 5&ndash;8 carefully chosen pieces get more inquiries than those with 20+ random clips. Quality signals expertise. Volume signals desperation.</li>
          <li><strong>Using Google Drive as your portfolio</strong> &mdash; Shared Google Drive folders require access requests, have no visual structure, and force the viewer to download videos to watch them. We have seen brand managers skip creator pitches entirely because the Drive link asked for permission. A dedicated portfolio page eliminates this friction completely.</li>
          <li><strong>Accepting the AI output without editing</strong> &mdash; AI-generated bios all start sounding the same. "Versatile content creator passionate about storytelling" could describe anyone. The AI gives you a draft. Your job is to inject personality, specifics, and your actual voice. Spend ten minutes editing what the AI produces.</li>
          <li><strong>Forgetting to update after new projects</strong> &mdash; Your portfolio is a living document, not a one-time project. If the most recent work on your page is six months old, brands assume you have stopped creating. Set a monthly reminder to review and refresh. With AI tools, adding a new project takes under two minutes.</li>
          <li><strong>No clear niche positioning</strong> &mdash; "I create content for any brand" is not a positioning statement. Brands hire specialists, not generalists. Even if you work across multiple categories, lead with one or two niches where you have the strongest work.</li>
        </ul>
